Josh Knarr wrote:	[Sun Mar 02 2003, 07:48:26PM EST]
> Bootstrap comparison failure!
> Cfgbuild.o differs
> Genextract.o differs
> Genrtl.o differs
> Read-rtl.o differs
> Rtl.o differs
> Tradcif.o differs
> Tree-dump.o differs
> Make[1]:  ***[compare-lean] Error 1
> Make[1]:  Leaving directory `/var/tmp/portage/gcc-3.2.2/work/build/gcc`
> Make:  *** [bootstrap-lean] Error 2

I think what's happening here is that gcc is attempting to bootstrap, so
it builds the lean compiler, then uses the lean compiler to build the
full-fledged compiler, then it's finding that the objects produced by
them differ.  Or something along those lines.

I'd suggest sticking the disk into the known-good system to build it in
a chroot, then put the disk back in the Miata.  Maybe if you get past
bootstrap and installation you'll actually have a working system.
Maybe.

> And...I'm stumped.  But the alpha IS reporting that it is a EV56 to
> /proc/cpu.  Telling it to build for EV5 stops the microcode complaints.

Yeah, it sounds like an old bug that's been reported on the kernel
lists.  Some Alpha hardware reports the wrong CPU.  I thought it was
fixed in recent kernels, but maybe if your hardware is screwy enough, it
doesn't matter.  ;-)

If you go ahead with the chroot idea, you could always instrument the
new kernel to report ev5 regardless.

> I was just brainstorming this to myself, what happens if the guy before
> me flashed the firmware with the wrong SRM+ARC?  Would this change the
> cpuid?  Lets assume that this is really a EV5 and that someone really
> did do this, unwittingly...  Where might I find the correct SRM for this
> machine?  (Lets also assume that it really is a 433a)...

http://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware/readme.html
http://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware/readmes/digitalpw.html

Aron

