wrote:

> Hi Ted,
> I have only subscribed to this list for a few days and couldn't find the 
> posted tips you refer to.
> Could you please send a copy of the mail to me (if you still have it)?
>
> -Goran
>

You can follow the x86 instructions pretty closely.  There is no liveCD 
except for rc_1, but it works fine.  Just make sure you know what type of 
processor it is when you modify your CFLAGS in /etc/make.conf

Some caveats: ---On your boot drive, make sure you leave the first two 
cylinders unpartitioned.  You must use a BSD disklabel if booting from SRM. 
 Start your /boot partition at cylinder #3.

---Be sure you have the line with "ACCEPT_KEYWORDS=~alpha" uncommented out 
of /etc/make.conf before you start stage2.

---The kernel sources to emerge are "emerge alpha-sources"

---In your kernel config, make sure to select "SRM as a bootloader" if 
booting from SRM (This is _required_ if you do not use the "generic" alpha 
type.  If you know your alpha type, its best to use that, as it will 
greatly speed up your kernel.)  You *MUST* also make sure the BSD Partition 
type is compiled into the kernel.  If you want to access /proc information 
from SRM, select that option as well.

---The bootloader is aboot.  Instead of emerging grub or lilo, emerge 
aboot.  (This is assuming you are booting from SRM.)  As far as installing 
the bootloader after you emerge it, read "man aboot", "man abootconf", and 
"man swriteboot".  You have to write the secondary bootstraploader to the 
boot disk using swriteboot, run abootconf to tell it how to find its conf 
files, and configure the /etc/aboot.conf file  "man aboot.conf".  I wish I 
had all the steps laid out, but I will try and do that this weekend and 
submit that to gentoo so no one else will have to be lost like I was.

---Emerging gentoo-stats does not work on alphas.


I hope this helps.

Ted

